# Expectation record for 02_constant_expressions/002_constant_expression_contexts.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 002_constant_expression_contexts
area               = 02_constant_expressions
description        = The four contexts C requires an integer constant expression in -- array bounds, case labels, bitfield widths and enumerator initializers -- each observed at run time and paired with a volatile-operand twin that recomputes the same value
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined behaviour and depends on no unspecified behaviour, which is the
precondition both differential oracles rest on rather than a stylistic preference: undefined
behaviour would license either compiler to do anything at all, so a divergence between them would
prove nothing about either. The paragraphs below are the written argument required of every program
in this suite; the warning gate and the sanitizer gate are its machine half.
EVERY ARRAY INDEX IS PROVABLY INSIDE ITS BOUND, and the bounds are the subject under test rather
than an assumption. Each loop is driven by the array's own extent computed as sizeof arr /
sizeof arr[0], so vec is walked over exactly its 10 elements, mat over exactly its 3 rows and 2
columns, wide over exactly its 8 elements and spanned over exactly its 7, and each end-value line
indexes only element 0 and element bound-1. The deliberate reads of the last element of every array
are there precisely to prove that the constant expression in the bound admitted the element count it
claimed. Because an index and its limit are derived from the SAME expression, a bound that folded
wrongly would change both together and be caught by the printed length rather than by an
out-of-range access, and thirteen _Static_assert declarations pin every bound, every enumerator
value and every bitfield width before the program runs, so a mis-folded constant expression is a
translation failure rather than an out-of-bounds access. No index is computed from an unbounded
quantity.
EVERY BITFIELD STORE IS INSIDE THE FIELD'S REPRESENTABLE RANGE, and that is asserted statically in
the source rather than left to inspection, so no implementation-defined truncation of an
out-of-range store can occur. The four-bit signed field is written with -8 and 7 -- and on the
runtime path with -(src & 7), which lies in [-7, 0], or with -8 or -1 explicitly, or with src & 7 in
[0, 7] -- the five-bit unsigned field only with src & 31u in [0, 31], and the three-bit unsigned tail
field only with src & 7u in [0, 7]. In each case those are the extremes of exactly what the declared
width can hold, which is what makes the width observable through values, and nothing relies on the
value of a field beyond its width.
EVERY SWITCH SELECTOR IS EITHER MATCHED BY A LABEL OR DELIBERATELY ROUTED TO default, so no dispatch
falls off the end of a switch without a defined destination, no path leaves the statement with a
value unaccounted for, and every case body ends in a return or a break. No case label is duplicated,
which the compiler would reject in any event.
EVERY SHIFT COUNT IS A SMALL NON-NEGATIVE CONSTANT, or a volatile object holding one, strictly below
the width of its promoted left operand -- 1 << 10, 1 << 4 through the WIDTH_BASE macro, one << shift
with shift equal to 4, and the two long long shifts by 33 and 30 against a 64-bit left operand. Every
shifted left operand is the positive literal 1 or 1LL, so only non-negative values are shifted left
and no negative value is right-shifted.
NO DIVISION IS UNDEFINED AND NO REMAINDER OPERATOR APPEARS. Every divisor in the file is one of
exactly three things, established by stripping comments and string literals and reading every
division that remains: the literal 2, in the WIDTH_TAIL and MAT_COLS macros, in the case labels
4 / 2 and 8 / 2, and in the two selector assignments that spell them; the constant 1LL << 30 inside
WIDE_LEN; and a sizeof of a complete object type, which the standard guarantees is non-zero. No
divisor is zero, none is negative, and no most-negative value is therefore ever divided by -1. Each
of those divisions is a constant expression the compiler evaluates at translation time, and the
remainder operator appears nowhere in the file at all -- also checked mechanically, and zero. NO
DIVISION IS PERFORMED AT RUN TIME: the twin of the wide quotient reaches 8 by adding 2^30 to an
accumulator eight times and comparing, never by dividing, which is the choice the wide-arithmetic
paragraph below explains.
THE WIDE ARITHMETIC CANNOT OVERFLOW. The widest int value the program handles at all is 4000, reached
as (int)sizeof(int) * 1000 in a case label and in the volatile selector that matches it; the widest
int value computed from opaque operands is 49, as 7 * 7 in the last element of spanned, and that same
49 is the widest int value printed as a value, because 4000 reaches stdout only inside the fixed
label text sparse_sel4000 rather than as a number. The two wide values are long long throughout,
written with LL-suffixed literals: 1LL << 33 == 8589934592 and 1LL << 30.
The 64-bit division twin is performed by repeated addition and comparison rather than by the division
operator, because every backend implements addition and comparison directly while 64-bit division may
go through a compiler support routine that is not this program's subject; eight additions of 2^30
reach exactly 2^33, so the quotient is 8 and the accumulated dividend is 8589934592 -- both printed
above, and both nowhere near the long long maximum, whose 64-bit minimum width means no overflow is
possible even on the 32-bit target. Plain long is never used.
NO POINTER IS FORMED ANYWHERE IN THIS PROGRAM and there is no pointer arithmetic beyond array
subscripting, so there is no aliasing rule to break, no one-past-the-end question to answer, no
object representation to inspect, no padding byte to observe and no dependence on the relative
addresses of unrelated objects. Every object is accessed only through its own declared type, and the
volatile struct is read and written only through its own members.
NO STORAGE IS READ UNINITIALIZED. Every automatic object is initialized in its own declaration, and
every object with static storage duration is zero-initialized before any read; vec, mat, wide and
spanned are fully written by their loops before their end values are read; live_bits is a volatile
static, so it starts zeroed, and each of its fields is assigned before the block that reads it; and
the elements printed as zero are exactly the ones no assignment reaches.
SEQUENCING IS EXPLICIT RATHER THAN INCIDENTAL. Each volatile object is read exactly once, into a
plain local, in a statement of its own; the arithmetic is then performed on the locals. The volatile
read is the optimization barrier, and the pattern keeps every full expression to a single side effect
and leaves every printf argument free of side effects, so the count and order of the volatile
accesses is fixed by statement boundaries rather than left unspecified. Every volatile increment --
the five walk counters -- stands alone in its own statement, and the loop counters compared against a
sizeof expression are read, not modified, inside the controlling expression. The two switch dispatch
lines are the one place worth naming: their two arguments are a constant-argument call and a
volatile-argument call to the SAME PURE function, which only switches on its parameter and returns,
so exactly one argument has an effect -- the volatile read -- and the other cannot be affected by it
in either evaluation order, which is what makes those lines independent of the unspecified order of
argument evaluation. No object is modified twice between two sequence points.
NO CONSTANT COMMA EXPRESSION APPEARS. A comma expression whose left operand has no side effect is
rejected by -Werror=unused-value under the default gate, and folding through the comma operator is
the subject of 006_conditional_and_comma_folding.c, which supplies side-effecting operands for it.
Every _Static_assert in the file is SATISFIED, which is a soundness requirement rather than a
preference: both mandated oracles compare binaries that were built successfully, so a deliberately
failing assertion would stop compilation on both sides and leave nothing to compare. Error
detection -- the correct rejection of an invalid program -- is a noted non-goal of this suite for
exactly that reason.
NO HEADER IS NAMED ANYWHERE and the single libc prototype the program needs, printf, is declared by
hand as `int printf(const char *, ...);`. bcc bundles the nine required freestanding headers plus a
bonus stdatomic.h and ships no standard I/O header (docs/project-guide.md line 212), so a directive
naming that header would fail on the bcc side of oracle (a) while succeeding on the reference side --
a spurious divergence caused by the test rather than by the compiler under test. _Static_assert is a
C11 keyword rather than a library facility, so the thirteen static assertions above need no header
either, and assert.h is deliberately not named: it would supply only the run-time macro, which is a
different construct from the translation-time declaration under test here. Nothing in the file is a
GCC extension, so no include exception and no gate deviation is available to area 02 and none is
needed; the corpus has exactly two header exceptions, area 07's stdarg.h and the dedicated
bundled-header probe in area 12.
THIS RECORD DECLARES NO WARNING-GATE DEVIATION: no ub_audit_flags key appears above, so the program
is audited under the suite's full default gate. It is clean under -Wall -Wextra -pedantic
-Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror at -O0, -O1 and -O2 with all four reference drivers,
and it builds and runs clean under the sanitizer gate -fsanitize=undefined,address
-fno-sanitize-recover=all run natively to completion. Both gates are reference-compiler-only and are
never applied to bcc, so requirement 3's shared-flag discipline is untouched.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This record narrows nothing. All four targets, all three optimization levels and all three
oracles are enabled, and no expected-divergence marker is attached. These notes exist to
record how that was achieved, because a program in this area could easily have had to narrow:
constant evaluation reaches straight into the type system, which is where the four supported
targets differ.
THE TWO-VARIANT RULE AND WHY BOTH HALVES ARE NEEDED. Every property is printed twice: once
from a constant expression, so the answer comes from the constant evaluator, and once from
volatile-qualified operands, which no implementation may read at translation time, so that
answer comes from the code generator. The mechanism was verified at instruction level rather
than assumed: volatile int x = 7; return x * 6; emits a genuine runtime multiply at -O2 where
the non-volatile form collapses to a single immediate move. Without the runtime half,
optimization substitutes the folder's answer for the backend's and a code-generation defect
escapes detection entirely while the suite still reports a pass. With it, a divergent fold_
line accuses the constant evaluator, a divergent runtime_ line accuses the backend, and a
pair that disagrees with itself accuses one of the two.
WIDTH NORMALIZATION. sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) were MEASURED at 4 bytes on i686 and 8
bytes on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64 (docs/technical-specifications.md target table, lines
457-462), so no plain long, no size_t, no ptrdiff_t and no pointer value is printed anywhere.
Where the program computes a value too wide for int, that value is written in long long with
LL-suffixed literals and printed with %lld, which is 64 bits on every enabled target; every
other integer reaches stdout through %d, with an explicit cast wherever the expression's own
type is an enumerator, a bitfield, wider or unsigned. No floating value reaches a format string.
Array bounds are consequently printed as ELEMENT-COUNT RATIOS -- sizeof of the array divided by
sizeof of its element -- and never as a sizeof in its own right. A ratio is target-invariant
where a byte count is not, and it is still the constant expression in the bound that determines
it, so no coverage is given up by the normalization.
PLAIN-char SIGNEDNESS. Plain char was MEASURED as signed on x86-64 and i686 and unsigned on
AArch64 and RISC-V 64. No plain-char value is printed and no printed line depends on that
choice.
BITFIELD LAYOUT IS DELIBERATELY NOT OBSERVED HERE, AND THAT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN A GAP.
C11 leaves the assignment of bitfields to allocation units, and whether a field may straddle a
unit boundary, implementation-defined. This program prints only the VALUES each declared width
can hold -- never sizeof(struct bits) and never _Alignof of it -- so no layout property reaches
stdout and no padding bit is ever observed, and the printed result follows from the width the
constant expression fixed rather than from the layout the ABI chose. The layout claim is owned by
04_bitfields/001_layout_and_size.c, which prints sizeof and _Alignof for five bitfield structs
and records that their sizes, alignments and byte images were MEASURED identical on all four
targets -- as were the read-back values of the sequence used here, including the signed field at
its minimum, -8 -- so a cross-backend divergence in these lines would be a genuine finding rather
than an implementation-defined difference. Duplicating area 04's layout assertion here would add
an implementation-defined layout property to a program whose subject is constant evaluation, and
no declared width goes untested as a result.
BITFIELD SIGNEDNESS IS FIXED IN THE SOURCE RATHER THAN INHERITED. A bitfield declared on
unqualified int has implementation-defined signedness, and this program prints its fields'
values, so the base types are written as explicitly signed int and unsigned int. The
zero-width unnamed member between them is the standard separator that ends the current
allocation unit; it is used for its layout effect only, and no value is printed for it, because
it has none.
THE SIZE OF AN ENUMERATED TYPE IS IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED and is neither asserted nor printed.
Only the enumerator VALUES appear, and those are fixed by the constant expressions that
initialize them, which is exactly the property this context is here to test.
ONE TARGET ASSUMPTION IS RELIED ON, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY LEFT UNASSERTED. The sparse switch
carries the label (int)sizeof(int) * 1000, so the line sparse_sel4000 exists only because
sizeof(int) is 4; C11 fixes only a minimum range for int, not its width, and 4 was MEASURED on
all four supported targets. Wrapping that in a _Static_assert would be the reflex, and it would
be wrong here: a failed static assertion refuses the whole translation unit and costs every other
observation in the cell, whereas leaving the fact to the dispatch means a target where int had
some other width shows up as exactly one differing line -- sparse_sel4000 -- with the other
fifty-three still compared. The choice is between one informative divergence and fifty-four
lost ones.
NOTHING HERE ASSERTS OR OBSERVES THE CODE SHAPE A BACKEND CHOOSES FOR A SWITCH. Label density is
the property a backend typically consults when it selects a dispatch strategy, and the dense and
sparse label sets below are written to put both cases in front of it, but which shape it picks --
a jump table or a comparison chain -- is at its discretion and is a property of the generated code
rather than of the printed result. The claim under test is that each selector value selects its own
label and yields its own result, whatever code is emitted and however that code differs between
targets or between optimization levels.
WHAT WOULD LEGITIMATELY BREAK THAT, AND HOW IT MUST THEN BE HANDLED. A conforming target with a
different sizeof(int) would print a different sparse_sel4000 line and a different
enum_shifted/bound_wide pair without being defective. That is an implementation-defined
divergence rather than a compiler defect, and it must then be recorded as an expected divergence
with this record's target list narrowed and the reason stated in this field -- never left to
surface as a finding against a backend that did nothing wrong, and never resolved by removing the
sizeof-derived case label, which is one of the constant-expression contexts requirement 2 asks
this program to cover.
EXECUTION CHARACTER SET: NOT ENGAGED, VERIFIED RATHER THAN ASSERTED. C11 5.2.1 leaves the
members of the execution character set and their codes implementation-defined, so a program
that printed a character's numeric code, or rendered a literal as bytes, would owe an
accounting here. This one owes none: it contains no character constant at all -- checked
mechanically, by stripping comments and string literals and finding zero -- and derives no
printed VALUE from a character. Its only string literals are printf format strings and, where
it declares any, _Static_assert messages -- the messages consumed by the compiler and never
emitted, the format strings contributing only fixed label text that is byte-identical in all
twelve cells of the matrix and in the golden above. There is therefore no code to pin and
nothing to narrow. That is precisely what distinguishes this program from 004_sizeof_alignof.c
and 007_string_literal_constants.c in the same area, both of which do print character codes and
both of which pin every code they print with a _Static_assert for that reason.
HERMETIC AND DETERMINISTIC. Every input is a literal in the source: nothing is read from the
clock, the environment, the command line, a file or a socket, and nothing is written anywhere.
Output is one line per semantic property claimed, in a fixed order, so a single divergent line
names the construct that produced it. No address or pointer value is printed, there is no
randomness, no locale-dependent formatting and no data-dependent iteration order, and the
program returns 0 -- well inside the 0-125 range the suite requires so that no exit status is
truncated by the operating system.
MEASURED EVIDENCE. The program was compiled with the reference driver for all four targets at
-O0, -O1 and -O2 -- twelve configurations -- and every one produced these 54 lines
byte-identically with exit status 0, which is why cross-backend comparison is sound, oracle (b)
is enabled with no narrowing, and oracle (c) can hold these bytes as the golden record.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
